In Oracle 9i, you can use the following query to check for blocking sessions:

```sql
SELECT
    s1.username || '@' || s1.machine || ' (SID=' || s1.sid || ')' AS "Blocked User",
    s2.username || '@' || s2.machine || ' (SID=' || s2.sid || ')' AS "Blocking User",
    s1.sql_hash_value,
    s1.status,
    s1.osuser,
    s1.machine,
    s1.module
FROM
    v$lock l1,
    v$session s1,
    v$lock l2,
    v$session s2
WHERE
    l1.sid = s1.sid
    AND l2.sid = s2.sid
    AND l1.BLOCK = 1
    AND l2.request > 0
    AND l1.id1 = l2.id1
    AND l2.id2 = l2.id2;
```

This query retrieves information about sessions that are blocking other sessions in the database.

To check for locking in Oracle 9i, you can use the following query:

```sql
SELECT
    s.username AS "Username",
    s.sid AS "Session ID",
    s.serial# AS "Serial Number",
    s.osuser AS "OS User",
    s.machine AS "Machine",
    s.module AS "Module",
    s.program AS "Program",
    l.type AS "Lock Type",
    l.lmode AS "Lock Mode",
    l.request AS "Request",
    o.object_name AS "Object Name"
FROM
    v$lock l
    JOIN v$session s ON l.sid = s.sid
    LEFT JOIN dba_objects o ON l.id1 = o.object_id
WHERE
    l.type <> 'MR'
    AND l.type <> 'AE';
```

This query retrieves information about sessions that are holding locks in the database, including details about the lock type, lock mode, requested mode, and the object name if applicable.

In Oracle 9i, you can use the following query to check for both blocking and locking sessions:

```sql
SELECT
    s1.username || '@' || s1.machine || ' (SID=' || s1.sid || ')' AS "Session",
    s1.osuser,
    s1.module,
    s1.status,
    l1.type AS "Lock Type",
    l1.id1,
    l1.id2,
    l1.request,
    l1.block
FROM
    v$session s1,
    v$lock l1
WHERE
    s1.sid = l1.sid
    AND (l1.request > 0 OR l1.block = 1);
```

This query retrieves information about sessions that are either holding locks (locking sessions) or being blocked (blocked sessions) in the database.



